  tasks 5 groups, notes 4, timeline 2, and every one resolves to a role split
  (caps labels 0.075/0.1/0.16em vs body 0em) or a sub-pixel delta (largest
  within-role gap 0.104px = 0.008em, "Add task"). No finding.
- contrast: all 21 sightings are 1:1 behind the opaque dictation floor. No finding.
- Filters: lane labels restate ("0 of 3 shown"), empty board offers one way out,
  Escape unwinds one filter per press with an honest sentence each time.
- Notes index dock band IS reserved (.index padding-bottom 132px); no row is
  eaten by the floating dock at 768/1280/1440/1920.
- The tick under the Add row at 1280 is a clipped-scroller artefact, not an
  overlap — .trayBody bottom 596, card top 604. Withdrawn.
- Timeline: editor opens after the plan, holds focus, steppers announce through
  .b-undo, Ctrl+Z works, Escape returns focus to the .b-grab that opened it.
- Undo strip pauses its own timer on focusin/mouseenter (l.4311-4314).

## VERDICT — 8.8

What earned it. This is a real application, not a document about one. The suite's
one load-bearing claim — three products mounted at once, nothing lost — is
measurable and it holds to the byte and to the pixel. The craft that automation
cannot see is largely at the benchmark: the contract keys are defended (Space on
role=checkbox ticks, and there is a comment saying why), the roving model reaches
every card, the filter model is the best thing on the surface (every lane label
restates what it still describes, the empty board offers exactly one way out,
Escape unwinds one filter per press and says so), the dictation floor traps focus
properly, and Notes and Timeline hold focus and every scroller through a full
1920-768-1920 sweep. The CSS reasons out loud; most non-obvious numbers carry the
measurement that produced them. Zero console errors in every state at five widths.

What stands between it and 9.5. Four small things and one that is not small.

The one that is not small is POLISH THAT IS ACTUALLY A BUG, half a day: the
keyboard carry has five exits and only Space records the move. Enter, Tab, a rail
switch and a pointer press elsewhere all commit a lane change that Ctrl+Z then
denies exists, and three of them leave the card painted picked-up with nothing
focused. The board's own file says this class was fixed and names Tab away
specifically; the fix reached the pointer only. It is one refactor —
commitCarry() — and four call sites.

The 1199-1420 lane slop is one CSS line (overflow-x: clip on .trayBody) — an hour
including a re-shoot of the 1280 frames.

The pace meter under a filter is a DECISION before it is a build: does the head
describe the project or the view? The rest of the sheet has already answered
"the view", so make the meter agree. Half a day.

The two accessibility ones are ten minutes each: one aria-hidden on the card's
describedby target, and one ternary deleted so a done card keeps saying it is
movable.

None of it is architecture, none of it is a direction, and none of it touches a
protected object. Call it a day and a half of work between this and 9.5 —
provided the carry fix is treated as the correctness bug it is and not as polish.
